---
title: "Shader cells"
---

A `{glsl}` cell is a fragment shader that draws to a live `<canvas>`. It is a *client-side
cell language*, the same kind of thing a `{js}` cell is: the browser is its kernel, so
nothing is executed at build time and nothing is vendored — WebGL is already in the page.

The cell body is the shader's `main`. Taliesin supplies the vertex shader and a small
preamble: `uv` (position in `0..1`, y up), `u_res` (canvas size in device pixels),
`u_time` (seconds since mount), and one `u_<name>` uniform per `//| input:` name.

{{< input name="freq" type="slider" min="2" max="40" step="1" value="14" label="frequency" >}}

```{glsl}
//| input: freq
void main() {
  vec2 p = (uv - 0.5) * 2.0;
  p.x *= u_res.x / u_res.y;
  float r = length(p);
  float v = 0.5 + 0.5 * sin(r * u_freq - u_time * 2.0);
  gl_FragColor = vec4(0.10 + v * 0.52, 0.09 + v * 0.46, 0.17 + v * 0.71, 1.0);
}
```

`u_time` is only wired up when the shader actually mentions it, so a static shader is drawn
once and then costs nothing. This one has no `u_time`, and redraws only when the slider moves:

```{glsl}
//| label: fig-gradient
//| fig-cap: A static shader, redrawn only when `frequency` changes.
//| input: freq
void main() {
  float band = fract(uv.x * u_freq * 0.25 + uv.y * 0.5);
  float soft = smoothstep(0.0, 0.5, band) * smoothstep(1.0, 0.5, band);
  vec3 tint = mix(vec3(0.11, 0.10, 0.18), vec3(0.62, 0.55, 0.86), soft);
  gl_FragColor = vec4(tint, 1.0);
}
```

@fig-gradient is a numbered figure like any other client-rendered float, because the
figure machinery asks the registry whether a language materializes at render time rather
than testing for `js` by name.
